encyclopedia :tovan do
  name "Tovan"
  summary "Tovan is an Istravan dueling practice in which opponents hold opposite ends of a sash while fighting with short hooked blades."
  kind :culture
  subkind :expressive_tradition
  status :complete
  topics :"social-structure", :danger, :military, :subject_istrava
  prevalence :uncommon
  appears_when any: { place: [:urban, :orbital, :yard] }
  log "2026-09-20 — Sparring Regimen suggested improvement through repeated encounters with another fighter. Extended tovan through borrowed equipment, exchanged techniques and different footing: https://mtg.wtf/card/stx/29/Sparring-Regimen."

  prose <<~PROSE
    In tovan, two fighters take opposite ends of a broad woven sash in their off hands. Each holds a short hooked blade in the other. They circle close enough to pull one another into reach. Releasing the sash concedes the bout. A hook can trap the cloth, turn a wrist or catch the other blade; a fighter who steps back without feeling the tension can be thrown forward onto the opponent's point.

    Tovan names the practice and an individual bout. Its sashes appear in orchard courts and bath terraces across Istrava. Shallow water is favored because it hides small changes of footing and makes a clean fall difficult. Dry floors reward faster steps. Ordinary contests use padded or dulled weapons and end at a marked touch, a disarm or a released sash.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :culture, heading: "Learning on Another Floor"
    Travelers carry tovan in a rolled sash with two practice hooks tucked inside. A shipboard bout needs steady gravity and room for both fighters to fall clear of furniture. Shortened sashes keep the pair inside a narrow floor but reward close wrist work; a bath-trained visitor can discover that a familiar retreat puts their back against a bulkhead. Crews suspend play when thrust or a changing local down makes the footing unreliable.

    Friends often exchange ends after a bout and repeat the decisive movement slowly. A skilled guest earns another invitation by teaching a useful escape as readily as by winning. The practice has spread among some Pell crews and Itharan lodgers through these visits. The broad steps and hidden footing of #{ref :tovanesh, "Tovanesh"} still draw players who learned with their heels against furniture and want to try a full court.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :culture, heading: "What Is Staked"
    Most bouts settle wagers and sporting rivalries. Grievance bouts begin with both parties stating what they offer if defeated. A borrowed room, a public retraction or the release of somebody held by one side can draw a larger audience than a purse. Seconds agree the stopping point and stand close enough to cut the sash. A party can decline before the cloth is taken; supporters may then refuse invitations, leave a patron's household or keep repeating the unanswered challenge.

    Promises rest on the people who hear them and on the challengers' power to keep them. A captain can offer a prisoner in the captain's custody. A boast about freeing an entire city attracts demands for particulars before the sash is handed over. Spectators sometimes choose to help enforce a broken promise, and sometimes go home while the winner argues alone.

    Sharp weapons are reserved for declared blood bouts. Physicians and seconds can refuse to assist. Some courts bar them entirely. A fighter may still conceal a sharpened edge among blunted hooks, turning a wound into an accusation that outlives the match.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :present_day, heading: "The Unwilling Hand"
    Aren's war has brought bound soldiers into grievance contests. Witnesses listen for repeated operative words and ask who can actually keep the promise offered. A fighter who freely wagers a possession may still be unable to release someone held under a standing command. Some courts refuse such matches; others accept them because the people seeking the captive see no better chance.

    Crowd favorites receive invitations from resistance parties as readily as from wealthy patrons. The honor of being asked can conceal a demand to fight again, farther from home, for stakes the fighter never offered. Experienced seconds teach their pupils to put the sash down before answering a new proposal.
  PROSE
  cue "The opponents take up a wet sash, and a second draws a cutting knife before letting them step into the pool."
  cue "A spectator demands that a captain name the captive being wagered; the crowd quiets to hear the answer."
  affordance "A public wager can put a concrete concession within reach of someone who lacks soldiers or political office."
  pressure "A defeated patron refuses the promised concession while the winner's supporters gather on the steps."
  variation "Bath courts conceal footwork in shallow water; dry orchard floors favor circling and sudden changes of direction."
  variation "Sporting bouts use padded hooks, while a declared blood bout brings sharp blades and seconds ready to sever the sash."
  variation "Ship crews shorten the sash to fit a clear floor; players visiting a bath court must learn to feel a step they can no longer see."
  affordance "Offering to repeat a winning movement slowly turns a contest into a lesson, giving an unfamiliar visitor a reason to be invited back."
